{
 "meta": {
  "purpose": "What belongs in each word field of the picture-description section, and which LOD words say where a thing is.",
  "selection": "The fields are selected by **English gloss**, not by writing Luxembourgish: `match` is a list of English words, and any A1/A2 vocabulary entry whose gloss contains one of them joins the field. English is the half of this app a person may write; the Luxembourgish is whatever LOD happens to have glossed that way. `add` and `drop` are LOD entry ids for the cases a gloss match cannot settle — `Boun` is glossed \"(green) bean\" and is not a colour, `Hiem` is glossed \"shirt\" and is. pipeline/build-picture.js refuses an id in either list that is not a real entry, so a wrong guess fails the build instead of silently doing nothing.",
  "positions": "The words that say where something is, named by entry id with the English this app shows. LOD's own gloss is used where it has one; several of the prepositions are published with no English at all, and those are glossed here — free text, like every other English string in the repo.",
  "placements": "Real LOD example sentences that place one thing relative to another, which is the sentence a picture description is made of. Named by the content hash build-picture.js gives them, so not one word of Luxembourgish is written here either; run `npm run build:picture -- --candidates` to see the pool with its ids.",
  "notSaid": "LOD publishes no word for the foreground — `Virdergrond` is not in the lexicon, though `Hannergrond` is. So this section teaches where things are with uewen/ënnen/lénks/riets/an der Mëtt and the prepositions, and does not reach for a foreground/background pair it cannot source.",
  "addedAt": "2026-10-04"
 },
